---
import TopicLayout from '../../layouts/TopicLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---
<TopicLayout
  title="Pinot Noir"
  subtitle="Red Grape · Burgundy, France"
  parentLabel="Grapes"
  parentPath={`${base}grapes/`}
  description="A comprehensive guide to Pinot Noir — its character, key regions, Burgundy's classification system, and the world's greatest producers."
  stats={[
    { value: 'Light–Medium', label: 'Body' },
    { value: 'High', label: 'Acidity' },
    { value: 'Low–Medium', label: 'Tannin' },
    { value: 'Pale ruby', label: 'Colour' },
    { value: 'Côte d\'Or', label: 'Heartland' },
    { value: '5–25+ years', label: 'Peak (Grand Cru)' },
  ]}
  toc={[
    { id: 'overview',        label: 'Overview' },
    { id: 'character',       label: 'Origins & Character' },
    { id: 'vineyard',        label: 'In the Vineyard' },
    { id: 'flavour',         label: 'Flavour Profile' },
    { id: 'regions',         label: 'Key Regions' },
    { id: 'classification',  label: 'Burgundy Classification' },
    { id: 'label',           label: 'Reading the Label' },
    { id: 'producers',       label: 'Notable Producers' },
    { id: 'price',           label: 'Price Guide' },
    { id: 'pairing',         label: 'Food Pairing' },
  ]}
>

  <!-- OVERVIEW -->
  <section id="overview" class="topic-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Overview</h2>
    <p class="section-lead">
      Pinot Noir is the most demanding and temperamental of the world's great red grape varieties. Its thin skin makes it vulnerable to rot, disease, and climate extremes. Its low tannin and high acidity leave nowhere to hide — every flaw in vineyard or cellar shows plainly in the glass. Yet in the right place, with the right producer, it produces wines of extraordinary complexity: silk-textured, fragrant, and capable of evolving over decades.
    </p>
    <div class="section-body">
      <p>It is the grape that made Burgundy's Côte d'Or one of the most contested and expensive pieces of agricultural land on earth. A Grand Cru vine from Romanée-Conti or Chambertin may produce just a few hundred bottles per year, each from a plot no larger than a garden. The argument that a single small parcel of land can produce something irreplaceable and unrepeatable — this is what Pinot Noir proved to the wine world.</p>
      <p>Winemakers around the world have spent the past fifty years trying to replicate what Burgundy does with this grape. Oregon, New Zealand's Central Otago, and California's Sonoma Coast have produced remarkable results. None have fully succeeded. The combination of Burgundy's ancient chalk and limestone soils, its marginal climate, and its centuries of viticultural knowledge remains, for now, irreducible.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ORIGINS & CHARACTER -->
  <section id="character" class="topic-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Origins & Character</h2>
    <p class="section-lead">One of the oldest cultivated grape varieties in existence, native to Burgundy, and parent to dozens of varieties now grown across the world.</p>
    <div class="section-body">
      <p>Pinot Noir has been cultivated in Burgundy for at least 1,400 years. Cistercian monks in the medieval period were instrumental in identifying and mapping the best vineyard sites — a process that shaped the classification system that still governs Burgundy today. The name "Pinot" derives from the pine-cone shape of the grape's tight, compact bunches.</p>
      <p>The variety mutates readily. "Pinot" refers to a family of clones rather than a single stable genotype. Pinot Gris and Pinot Blanc are colour mutations of the same variety. More significantly, Pinot Noir crossed with Gouais Blanc (now almost extinct) to produce Chardonnay — making it the parent of the world's most planted white grape.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="fact-list">
      <span class="fact-key">Origin</span>
      <span class="fact-val">Burgundy, France — cultivated for at least 1,400 years</span>
      <span class="fact-key">Family</span>
      <span class="fact-val">Pinot family; parent of Chardonnay, Pinot Gris, Pinot Blanc, Pinot Meunier</span>
      <span class="fact-key">Skin</span>
      <span class="fact-val">Thin — high susceptibility to disease and climate damage</span>
      <span class="fact-key">Bunch</span>
      <span class="fact-val">Small, tight, pine-cone shaped — increases disease pressure</span>
      <span class="fact-key">Ripening</span>
      <span class="fact-val">Early — among the first red grapes to ripen each harvest</span>
      <span class="fact-key">Permitted in</span>
      <span class="fact-val">Burgundy, Champagne, Alsace, Loire, Germany (Spätburgunder), Oregon, California, New Zealand, and many others</span>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- IN THE VINEYARD -->
  <section id="vineyard" class="topic-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">In the Vineyard</h2>
    <p class="section-lead">Pinot Noir is often called the "heartbreak grape" by those who grow it. It demands the closest site selection of any major variety — and then demands equally close attention throughout the growing season.</p>
    <div class="cards-grid cards-grid-2">
      <div class="info-card">
        <p class="info-card-meta">Key challenge</p>
        <h3 class="info-card-title">Climate Sensitivity</h3>
        <p>Pinot Noir requires a cool climate to retain its defining acidity and aromatic complexity. Too warm, and the red fruit turns jammy, the freshness disappears, the wine loses its distinctiveness. Even moderate warming between vintages produces a noticeably different style.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="info-card">
        <p class="info-card-meta">Key challenge</p>
        <h3 class="info-card-title">Disease Pressure</h3>
        <p>Thin skin and tight bunches create high susceptibility to botrytis (grey rot), powdery mildew, and downy mildew. Wet growing seasons — common in Burgundy — can devastate a crop. Vineyard management decisions in spring and summer are critical.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="info-card">
        <p class="info-card-meta">Key challenge</p>
        <h3 class="info-card-title">Early Budding</h3>
        <p>Pinot Noir buds early in spring, exposing young shoots to late frost — a persistent threat in Burgundy. The village of Chablis lost more than half its crop to frost in both 2016 and 2021. Frost management (smudge pots, wind machines, sprinkler systems) is now a significant investment for serious producers.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="info-card">
        <p class="info-card-meta">Key requirement</p>
        <h3 class="info-card-title">Low Yields</h3>
        <p>Concentration and complexity in Pinot Noir requires restricted yields. Grand Cru Burgundy is typically harvested at 25–35 hectolitres per hectare. Regional Bourgogne may be twice that. The trade-off is direct: lower yields, deeper wine — and significantly higher cost of production.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- FLAVOUR PROFILE -->
  <section id="flavour" class="topic-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Flavour Profile</h2>
    <p class="section-lead">Pinot Noir is a wine of paradox. It is pale in colour but profound in flavour. It has low tannin but exceptional structure. It is delicate and persistent simultaneously.</p>
    <div class="flavour-grid">
      <div class="flavour-col">
        <p class="flavour-heading">Young</p>
        <ul class="flavour-list">
          <li>Raspberry</li>
          <li>Fresh cherry</li>
          <li>Strawberry</li>
          <li>Red plum</li>
          <li>Violet, rose petal</li>
          <li>Forest floor (sous bois)</li>
          <li>Subtle spice (cinnamon, allspice)</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="flavour-col">
        <p class="flavour-heading">With Age</p>
        <ul class="flavour-list">
          <li>Dried cherry, pomegranate</li>
          <li>Wild mushroom</li>
          <li>Autumn leaves, damp earth</li>
          <li>Dried rose, pot-pourri</li>
          <li>Game, leather</li>
          <li>Truffle</li>
          <li>Soy, umami</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="flavour-col">
        <p class="flavour-heading">Structure</p>
        <ul class="flavour-list">
          <li>Colour: pale ruby to garnet</li>
          <li>Body: light to medium</li>
          <li>Tannin: low to medium, silky</li>
          <li>Acidity: high, the backbone</li>
          <li>Alcohol: 12–14%</li>
          <li>Finish: long, savory, persistent</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="section-body" style="margin-top:1.25rem;">
      <p>The texture is Pinot Noir's most distinctive quality: a transparency and silkiness that no other red grape replicates. Great Burgundy feels as if it dissolves rather than coats the palate. This characteristic, combined with the wines' aromatic complexity and longevity, is what drives the extraordinary prices commanded by the finest producers.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- KEY REGIONS -->
  <section id="regions" class="topic-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Key Regions</h2>
    <p class="section-lead">Pinot Noir is cultivated across the world, but it achieves distinctive, serious quality in only a handful of locations. Soil, climate, and altitude all matter — none more than in Burgundy.</p>

    <!-- Côte d'Or SVG Map -->
    <div class="map-container">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 340 560" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="region-map" aria-label="Schematic map of the Côte d'Or, Burgundy">

        <!-- Escarpment edge (west side) -->
